, and BD has equation y=x. Hence I=(2/5,6/5) and H=(2/3,2/3). The required area is [ABH]−[AEI]=2/3−1/5=7/15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1991_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-intersections","label":"Find intersections in coordinates","nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_p077","cnm_055","cnm_082","cnm_089","cnm_p173"],"id":"solution:coordinate-intersections:b5ee581178e3","label":"Find the intersections with coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-intersections":["cnm_p150","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_p148","cnm_p077","cnm_055","cnm_082","cnm_089","cnm_p173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (5√5/3)","reason":"Take A = (0,3), B = (6,0), C = (4,2), and D = (2,0). Lines AB and CD have equations y = 3 - x/2 and y = x - 2, giving E = (10/3,4/3). Hence AE = √((10/3)² + (-5/3)²) = 5√5/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2000_AMC_10_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-intersection","label":"Line equations on the unit lattice","nodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_089","cnm_075","cnm_110"],"id":"solution:coordinate-intersection:fe03dc3a79a4","label":"Find the intersection using coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-intersection":["cnm_082","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_174","cnm_089","cnm_075","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 6 solutions","reason":"The bound graph has domain [-7,5]. Its only inputs giving output 6 are -2 and 1, both in that domain. Thus f(f(x))=6 means f(x)=-2 or f(x)=1. The level -2 crosses the left and right outer segments, giving x=-6 and 11/3. The level1 crosses all four segments, giving x=-9/2,-1/3,1/6,8/3. All six are distinct and in [-7,5], and their inner outputs -2 or 1 remain in the outer domain. Source D is 6.","recordedAnswer":"D","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"piecewise-linear-preimages","label":"Solve on the four line segments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"piecewise-linear-preimages":["cnm_205","cnm_042","cnm_195"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_205","cnm_042","cnm_195"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 20","reason":"Triangles GHC and GAE are similar with ratio HC/AE = 3/5. Writing GF = h, their corresponding heights give (h - 8)/h = 3/5, so h = 20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-line-intersection","label":"Find the intersection using coordinates","nodeIds":["cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_084"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_055","cnm_064","cnm_042"],"id":"solution:coordinate-line-intersection:f37d99256e84","label":"Find G using equations of lines","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-line-intersection":["cnm_065","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_065","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_186","cnm_055","cnm_064","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 25/2","reason":"FG = 5 - 1 - 2 = 2. If E has height h above AB, similarity of EFG and EAB gives (h - 3)/h = 2/5, hence h = 5. The area is 5·5/2 = 25/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-line-intersection","label":"Calculate E using coordinates","nodeIds":["cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_084"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_055","cnm_082","cnm_042"],"id":"solution:coordinate-line-intersection:7c1a51ebc6d9","label":"Method 2: Coordinates and line equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-line-intersection":["cnm_p148","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_186","cnm_055","cnm_082","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 25/2","reason":"FG = 5 − 1 − 2 = 2. If H is E's height above AB, similarity gives (H − 3)/H = 2/5, hence H = 5. Therefore the area is (1/2)(5)(5) = 25/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-intersection","label":"Find E from two line equations","nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_055","cnm_082","cnm_042"],"id":"solution:coordinate-intersection:184d9cab63fe","label":"Find the intersection using coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-intersection":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_p148","cnm_p077","cnm_055","cnm_082","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 88/5 square units","reason":"Since AH is parallel to CG, triangles AHM and GCM are similar, giving AM:MG = 12:8 = 3:2. Thus area(ACM) = (3/5)area(ACG) = (3/5)·16. Adding area(ABC) = 8 gives area(ABCM) = 88/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_10A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinate-line-intersection","label":"Coordinates and intersecting lines","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-line-intersection":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_201","cnm_089"],"id":"coordinates-intersection-areas","label":"Method 2: Coordinates and triangle areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinates-intersection-areas":["cnm_p150","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_p148","cnm_201","cnm_089"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 1/5 square unit","reason":"Place the bottom-left vertex at (0,0), so A=(0,2) and B=(1,2). The two segments lie on y=2-x/2 and y=2x, giving C=(4/5,8/5). Its distance below AB is 2/5, so the area is (1/2)×1×(2/5) = 1/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_10A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-line-intersection","label":"Coordinate line intersection","nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_112","cnm_082","cnm_201","cnm_042"],"id":"solution:coordinate-line-intersection:cae561b8403b","label":"Find the intersection with coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-line-intersection":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_p148","cnm_p077","cnm_112","cnm_082","cnm_201","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 58","reason":"The quadrilateral has area 15/2. For E=(x,y), triangle ADE has area 2y=15/4, so y=15/8. Since CD has equation y=12-3x, x=27/8. Both fractions are reduced, giving 27+8+15+8=58.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"shoelace-and-line-equation","label":"Shoelace and the side equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shoelace-and-line-equation":["math:shoelace-formula","cnm_205","cnm_196"]},"nodeIds":["math:shoelace-formula","cnm_205","cnm_196"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 1/6","reason":"Take D=(0,0), C=(1,0), A=(0,2), B=(1,2), F=(1/2,0), and H=(1/2,1). The side intersections are (1/3,2/3) and (2/3,2/3). Each shaded triangle has base HF=1 and altitude 1/6, so their total area is 2·(1/2)·1·(1/6) = 1/6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_10A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-intersections","label":"Coordinate intersections","nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p077","cnm_p148","cnm_042"],"id":"solution:coordinate-intersections:da31fbbdc545","label":"Find the intersections with coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-intersections":["cnm_p150","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_055","cnm_p077","cnm_p148","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 10/91","reason":"Extend AG to meet line CD at H beyond C. Similarity gives CH=5/3, hence FH=14/3. Since AE=4 and CF=3, EP/EF=4/(4+3)=4/7. Similarly, EQ/EF=4/(4+14/3)=6/13. Thus PQ/EF=4/7-6/13=10/91.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-line-intersections","label":"Coordinate line intersections","nodeIds":["cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_h173"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_089","cnm_055","cnm_p210"],"id":"solution:coordinate-line-intersections:3decb046f863","label":"Use line equations and horizontal separations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-line-intersections":["cnm_268","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_268","cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_174","cnm_082","cnm_089","cnm_055","cnm_p210"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3 square units","reason":"Let X=EB∩CF. Triangles BCX and FEX are similar with base ratio 1:3, so their heights are 1 and 3 because the rectangle's height is 4. Each wing has area (1·4)/2−(1·1)/2=3/2, giving total area 3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_8_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinate-line-intersection","label":"Coordinate intersection","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-line-intersection":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 108","reason":"Since AB is parallel to CE and AB=2CE, similarity gives AF=2CF. For square side s, F is therefore s/3 above CD. Thus area(FCE)=(1/2)(s/2)(s/3)=s²/12, and area(AFED)=s²/2−s²/12=5s²/12=45. Hence s²=108.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_8_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinate-intersection","label":"Coordinates and line intersection","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-intersection":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 50","reason":"Write a=f(300)=f(900). The supplied inequality gives f(400),f(800) in [a-50,a+50], using the nearer endpoint 300 for 400 and 900 for 800. Thus |f(800)-f(400)|<=100, and applying the same supplied inequality to the two inner values gives f(f(800))-f(f(400))<=50. For attainment interpolate linearly through (-50,-25),(50,25),(300,0),(400,-50),(800,50),(900,0), and extend constantly outside that range. The consecutive slopes are 1/2,-1/10,-1/2,1/4,-1/2, all between-1/2 and 1/2. For any x<y, split[x,y] at these finitely many breakpoints. Each piece has increment between-Delta/2 and Delta/2; adding and telescoping proves the required global inequality, including points in different pieces. The construction has f(300)=f(900)=0, f(800)=50 and f(400)=-50, giving f(50)-f(-50)=25-(-25)=50. The greatest value is 50, B.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"supplied-bound-and-piecewise-attainment","label":"Use the supplied difference bound and construct an attaining piecewise-linear function","methodDirectNodeIds":{"supplied-bound-and-piecewise-attainment":["cnm_h018","cnm_h031","cnm_205","math:function-composition-iteration"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h018","cnm_h031","cnm_205","math:function-composition-iteration"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (5/3)","reason":"Since (2+i)²=3+4i, the two points are (2,1) and (3,4). Their line is y=3x−5. A real point has y=0, giving r=5/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cartesian-line-intercept","label":"Cartesian line and real intercept","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cartesian-line-intercept":["cnm_205"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_205"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: √3/2","reason":"Set A = (0,0), B = (s,0), D = (0,s), where s² = 1 + √3. Then AE is y = √3x and BD is x + y = s. Thus y_F = √3s/(1 + √3), and [ABF] = √3s²/(2(1 + √3)) = √3/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-intersection","label":"Intersect AE and BD in coordinates","nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_084"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_191","cnm_174"],"id":"solution:coordinate-intersection:9d8b1a4aa762","label":"Find the intersection using coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-intersection":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_208","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_205","cnm_2